.TH SAPHIRA-IMAGE 8 "2026-09-26" "Saphira Linux" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
saphira\-image \- build a bootable Saphira disk image
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B saphira\-image
.B build
.B \-\-target
.I TARGET
.B \-\-output
.I IMAGE
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B saphira\-image
.B list\-targets
.br
.B saphira\-image
.B plan
.B \-\-target
.I TARGET
.RI [ \-\-size
.IR SIZE ]
.br
.B saphira\-image
.B report
.B \-\-target
.I TARGET
.RI [ \-\-role
.IR ROLE ]
...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B saphira\-image
partitions a disk image, installs Saphira into it through the canonical
installer, prepares the requested init system, installs a bootloader, and
reports what went in.
.PP
It supports eight targets, covering both init systems, both boot
methods, and both the minimal and the product profiles:
.PP
.RS
.TS
l l l.
bios\-openrc     BIOS,   OpenRC,  minimal
bios\-systemd    BIOS,   systemd, minimal
bios\-saphira    BIOS,   OpenRC,  product
bios\-saphirad   BIOS,   systemd, product
uefi\-openrc     UEFI,   OpenRC,  minimal
uefi\-systemd    UEFI,   systemd, minimal
uefi\-saphira    UEFI,   OpenRC,  product
uefi\-saphirad   UEFI,   systemd, product
.TE
.RE
.PP
A target is a row in
.IR files/targets ,
not an implementation. Assembly is common; only the partition plan, the
bootloader invocation and the init preparation differ.
.SH USAGE
The tool is a one-shot administrative command and never runs as a service.
It needs root, because it creates loop devices and mounts filesystems, and
it must run on a host that has the partitioning and formatting tools
available: util-linux for sfdisk and losetup, e2fsprogs for mkfs.ext4,
dosfstools for mkfs.vfat, and grub for grub-install. qemu is needed only for
a .qcow2 output.
.PP
.BR saphira\-image " plan" ", " list\-targets " and " report
are static: they need neither root nor the assembler, so a partition plan
can be inspected on a machine that could not build an image.
.SH ARCHITECTURE
Ownership is split deliberately, so that there is exactly one assembler
and exactly one source of dependency truth:
.TP
.B saphira\-image
the target matrix, the partition plan, the bootloader invocation, init
preparation, and measurement.
.TP
.BR saphira\-install (8)
the mounted\-target installation.
.TP
.BR saphira\-assemble\-rootfs (8)
rootfs assembly and the
.B accounts.d
/
.B sysusers.d
identity replay.
.TP
APK metadata
the dependency closure. This tool never enumerates a resolved closure.
.PP
This tool never unpacks an APK, never writes a package list of its own,
and will not substitute its own installer for
.BR saphira\-install .
.SH INIT SELECTION
The init system is an explicit operator choice carried by the target. It
is never detected.
.PP
Packages ship BOTH init definitions unconditionally; unit files are inert
where systemd is not installed and OpenRC scripts are inert where it is.
What differs between an
.B \-openrc
and a
.B \-systemd
image is which set is
.IR enabled ,
and whether the boot path is consistent with what is on disk. Nothing
here infers an active init from installed files, and nothing teaches APK
installation to guess.
.PP
For an OpenRC target,
.B /etc/inittab
is checked to select
.BR /sbin/openrc ,
and enabled services are written as
.I /etc/runlevels/default
symlinks rather than by invoking
.BR rc\-update ,
which expects a running init.
.PP
For a systemd target,
.B default.target
points at
.BR multi\-user.target ,
and services are enabled offline with
.BR "systemctl \-\-root=/ enable" .
Nothing is ever started: an image has no running init, and enabling is a
persistent intent.
.SH PHYSICAL DISKS
.B "saphira\-image install\-to\-disk \-\-target"
.I TARGET
.B \-\-device
.I DEV
installs through the same engine onto a real disk instead of an image
file: the same partition generator (sized to the whole device), the
same installer and identity replay, the same init preparation and
service enablement, the same PARTUUID-stable bootloader, the same
measurement. Only the backing differs, and the bootloader targets the
whole disk rather than a loop device.
.PP
There is no default device and no discovery. The tool refuses a
missing path, a non-block file, a partition (it owns the whole table),
any disk with a mounted partition or active swap, and the disk hosting
the running system. The complete plan is always printed first;
.B \-\-preview
prints it and stops without touching the device, and a real run
requires the operator to type the device path back before anything is
partitioned. On failure the disk is unmounted and left as-is for
diagnosis; nothing is wiped by the error path.
.PP
UEFI installs use the removable fallback bootloader path
.RB ( BOOTX64.EFI ),
so no NVRAM entry is written and installation works where firmware
variables are unreachable. Writing a boot entry with
.B efibootmgr
remains a deliberate follow-up, not a silent requirement.
.SH PARTITION PLANS
The two plans are genuinely different and neither is derived from the
other by arithmetic.
.TP
.B bios
GRUB's
.I core.img
must land somewhere the BIOS can find at boot. On a GPT layout that
means a dedicated
.B bios\-boot
partition, not the post\-MBR gap a DOS layout would use. There is no ESP:
a BIOS system has no firmware to read one.
.TP
.B uefi
The firmware reads a FAT EFI System Partition. BIOS partitioning rules
are deliberately not reused: a
.B bios\-boot
partition means nothing to UEFI firmware, and without a real ESP the
firmware finds no bootloader at all. The ESP defaults to 64 MiB
.RB ( \-\-esp\-size )
and is always formatted FAT32, mounted at
.IR /boot/efi ,
and
.B grub\-install
writes its payload there.
.PP
The first partition begins at sector 2048 for 1 MiB alignment, and
.B saphira\-image
refuses to emit a plan whose last sector exceeds the requested image
size. The plan is generated, never hand-written per target, and
.B saphira\-image plan
prints it for inspection without building anything.
.SH PROFILES AND ROLES
A profile lists
.I intentions
only. Dependency closure belongs to APK metadata and is never duplicated
into an image definition.
.PP
The minimal profiles carry exactly what a bootable, manageable machine
needs, and each entry is justified in the profile file itself. Role
layers add intentions on top. A role is profile data, not a meta\-package:
no new package name is invented for a role, and the package manager still
owns the closure.
.SH MEASUREMENT
.B saphira\-image report
and the end of every build report the package count, rootfs size, image
size and the largest packages in the payload. These figures are reported
for visibility, not as a target. No functionality is removed to improve
them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-target " NAME"
One of the eight targets.
.TP
.BI \-\-output " PATH"
Image path. A
.B .raw
suffix produces a raw image; a
.B .qcow2
suffix produces a compressed one and requires
.BR qemu\-img .
An existing path is refused rather than overwritten.
.TP
.BI \-\-size " SIZE"
Minimum image size, accepting
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
.BR G ,
.BR T .
Defaults to 2G.
.TP
.BI \-\-allocate " SIZE"
How much of the capacity the initial partition layout occupies: an
absolute size or a percentage of
.BR \-\-size .
The remainder stays unallocated for later growth. Omitted, the
partitions occupy the whole capacity.
.TP
.BI \-\-esp\-size " MIB"
ESP capacity in MiB: one of 64, 128, 256, 512, 768, 1024.
Defaults to 64. Below 64 is refused: FAT32 needs at least 65,525
data clusters, so a smaller ESP cannot be valid FAT32. Larger sizes
suit Windows coexistence and UKIs; the formatter stays FAT32 at
every permitted size.
.TP
.BI \-\-root\-size " SIZE"
Exact root partition capacity, accepting
.BR M ,
.BR G ,
.BR T
(1MiB-aligned, at least 32MiB). Omitted, root fills the allocation.
With
.BR \-\-allocate ,
root plus the fixed partitions must fit inside it; over-budget
combinations are refused, nothing is resized silently, and the
remainder stays unallocated. Under
.BR \-\-rootfs " zfs"
this sizes the pool partition; /boot is sized by
.BR \-\-boot\-size .
.TP
.BI \-\-rootfs " FS"
Root filesystem: ext4 (default) or zfs. A zfs root is a pool with a
separate ext4 /boot (GRUB reads files, never pool features); the
target must carry the ZFS kmod and userspace (add
.BR \-\-role " role-zfs"
or an equivalent profile). Hybrid boot with zfs is refused.
.TP
.BI \-\-pool " NAME"
ZFS pool name (default tank). Refused when a pool of that name
already exists on the building host.
.TP
.BI \-\-boot\-size " MIB"
ext4 /boot capacity for zfs roots in MiB: one of 128, 256 (default),
512, 1024. Ignored for ext4 roots.
.TP
.BI \-\-repository " PATH"
Repository index to install from. Defaults to the host repository.
The installer appliance finalizes its own
.I /etc/apk/repositories
to the public repository.
.TP
.BI \-\-hostname " NAME"
Hostname written into the image. Defaults to
.BR saphira
on OpenRC targets and
.BR saphirad
on systemd targets: product identity runs down to the default
hostname.
.TP
.BI \-\-root\-password " PW"
Root password set inside the image. If omitted, root is locked and
ordinary firstboot owns account setup; a supplied password is for
automated boot-test images only.
.TP
.BI \-\-role " NAME"
Add a role layer. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-role\-file " PATH"
Add intentions from a file. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-enable
Install the profile but enable none of its services.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-work
Retain the scratch workspace when a build fails, for diagnosis.
.TP
.B install\-to\-disk \-\-target
.I TARGET
.B \-\-device
.I DEV
Install onto a physical disk. Accepts
.BR \-\-preview ,
.BR \-\-allocate ,
.BR \-\-esp\-size ,
.BR \-\-root\-size ,
.BR \-\-hostname ,
.BR \-\-root\-password ,
.BR \-\-kernel\-arg ,
.BR \-\-arch ,
.BR \-\-repository ,
.BR \-\-netconf ,
.BR \-\-role ,
.BR \-\-role\-file ,
.BR \-\-no\-enable ,
.BR \-\-keep\-work .
There is no
.B \-\-output
or
.B \-\-size :
the disk's own capacity is the size.
.PP
Before partitioning, every existing signature on the disk (partition
tables, filesystem superblocks, RAID and LVM metadata) is wiped with
.B wipefs \-a .
The confirmed operation is whole-disk destruction, and a stale
signature left behind would confuse probing or stall partitioning
waiting for a confirmation it will never receive.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero on success. Non\-zero on any refusal. A failed build removes the
partial image it created; a pre\-existing output path is never touched.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SAPHIRA_TMPDIR
Scratch location. Defaults to
.BR /build/tmp .
Saphira tool scratch never uses host
.BR /tmp .
.TP
.B SAPHIRA_INSTALL
Override the
.B saphira\-install
path.
.TP
.B SAPHIRA_CHROOT
Override the
.B saphira\-chroot
path.
.TP
.B SAPHIRA_IMAGE_PROFILES
Override the profile tree the tool reads. Defaults to the shared tree
.RI ( /usr/share/saphira/profiles ,
or the install-tools source tree when run from a recipe checkout).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/saphira-image
the tool.
.TP
.I /usr/share/saphira/image/targets
the target matrix: one row per supported boot target, each naming a boot
method, an init system and a profile. This is data, so adding a target is a
line rather than a new implementation.
.TP
.I /usr/share/saphira/profiles/
the single authoritative profile tree, owned by saphira-install-tools
.RB ( saphira-profiles (5)).
The image tool reads it (installed package, source tree, or
.B SAPHIRA_IMAGE_PROFILES
override) and stages what each build needs into scratch; it ships no
profile namespace of its own. A profile lists
.I intentions
only; dependency closure belongs to APK metadata. Profiles may
.B @include
other profiles; includes resolve inside the staged scratch tree and a
missing include fails the build rather than guessing.
.TP
.I /usr/share/saphira/image/services.d/
OPTIONAL package-to-service mapping, one file per package, one init
service or unit base name per line. A package normally names its service
after itself, so an absent directory means every service is called after
its package. The exception is real:
.BR saphira\-packager " ships " saphira\-build ,
not
.BR saphira\-packager ,
so assuming the two names match enabled nothing at all, silently. The
mapping is data beside the profiles rather than something a profile
names, because a profile lists package
.I intentions
only
.RB ( saphira-profiles (5));
enumerating a package's services in a profile would duplicate package
knowledge in the image definition and rot whenever the package changes.
.TP
.I /etc/saphira/
controller configuration, owned by saphira-packager.
.SH NOTES
Neither
.B saphira\-install
nor
.B saphira\-assemble\-rootfs
is modified by this package, and neither is duplicated here. If the
canonical path cannot express something the image needs, the correct change
is to those tools and not a second assembler here.
.PP
Packages continue to ship BOTH init definitions. The image creator chooses
which set is enabled; it does not change what a package ships, and no
package is rebuilt for a different init.
.PP
The reported sizes are measurements, not targets. Removing functionality to
improve them would be the wrong trade for a server image.
.SH EXAMPLES
Inspect a partition plan without building anything:
.PP
.RS
.nf
saphira\-image plan \-\-target uefi\-systemd \-\-size 8G
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build a minimal UEFI/systemd server image:
.PP
.RS
.nf
saphira\-image build \-\-target uefi\-systemd \-\-size 8G \\
    \-\-output saphira-server.qcow2 \-\-hostname srv1 \\
    \-\-role role\-networking
.fi
.RE
.SH AUTHOR
AKADATA LIMITED, for Saphira Linux.
